{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001 substitutes an architectural proposal (expert + dynamic checklist + panel) for that evidentiary criterion. Its only offered discriminator — whether the checklist is static (mere compliance) versus a fully specified optimal state-transition operator (genuine gain) — is a property of the checklist's design, not an observable outcome within an evaluation. Knowing the checklist is 'dynamic' or 'fully specifies the optimal operator' does not separate cases where panel adherence is causally responsible for correctness from cases where the checklist happens to encode the right answers for the tested items but would fail under distribution shift. The target provides no single-evaluation evidence (e.g., item-level error analysis, held-out transfer, agreement-decomposition) that would let an evaluator tell the two apart; it asserts the distinction is architectural rather than demonstrating how to detect it empirically.","counterexample":[{"evaluation":"A single evaluation in which the dynamic checklist yields 95% accuracy on the tested items, but item-level analysis shows every panel error occurs exactly where the expert's rule was ambiguous or under-specified, while a static baseline checklist with no expert input scores 92% — the 3-point gap cannot be attributed to 'dynamic vs static' without further evidence separating rule-quality from panel-execution fidelity."},{"evaluation":"Two checklists, one static and one dynamic, both score 90%; a held-out transfer set shows the static one drops to 70% while the dynamic one holds at 88%. This transfer result is the within-evaluation evidence that distinguishes genuine gain, but SRC_001 never identifies it."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}